# Sentinel — Agent Operating Rules

Sentinel is an automated electronic climbing belay device ("Smart Belay" /
"Electric Grigri"): a mechatronic system built on ESP32 (C++ / PlatformIO)
that reads a rotary encoder, load cell, and IMU in real time to control a
servo/BLDC motor mechanism, managing rope slack and triggering a fast-acting
emergency brake on falls. This is a Master's Thesis project in Industrial
Engineering at NTNU Trondheim, supervised by Amund Skavhaug.

These rules apply to every agent session working in this repository and
supersede default behavior when they conflict.

## 1. Read documentation before acting

Before making ANY code or system change (firmware, wiring, build config,
project structure, etc.), you MUST first read:

- `docs/DEV_LOG.md` — the running history of decisions and changes.
- Any relevant files under `docs/` (technical specs, pinouts, datasheets)
  that relate to the area you are about to touch.

Do not propose or make changes based on assumptions when this context is
available and unread.

## 2. Log every change in `docs/DEV_LOG.md`

Upon completing any task that changes the project (code, structure, wiring,
architecture decisions, tooling, etc.), you MUST append an entry to
`docs/DEV_LOG.md` describing:

- Date.
- What changed and why (short rationale).
- Any follow-up items or open questions.

Never end a task silently — the log is the source of truth for project
history.

## 3. Ask before acting on ambiguity

If you encounter ambiguity, missing information, or a choice between
multiple valid design/implementation approaches (e.g. pin assignments,
control algorithms, library choices, mechanical tradeoffs), you MUST stop
and ask the user before proceeding. Do not make unilateral design decisions
on their behalf.

## 4. Documentation separation

- `docs/` — general technical specs, pinouts, hardware datasheets, and
  non-academic engineering documentation.
- `docs/thesis/` — exclusively academic Master's Thesis documents written in
  LaTeX (`.tex`). Do not place non-thesis documentation here, and do not
  place thesis chapters outside this folder.

## 5. Do not run `git add` / `git commit` / `git push`

The user performs all git staging, committing, and pushing themselves. Do
not run these commands on their behalf, even after making file changes.
You may still run read-only git commands (e.g. `git status`, `git diff`,
`git log`) to inspect state when useful.

## 6. Do not compile the thesis to PDF unless strictly necessary

The user exports/compiles the LaTeX thesis documents to PDF themselves. Do
not run `pdflatex`/`biber`/etc. to produce or update a PDF unless it is
extremely necessary to verify that a change actually compiles (e.g. after a
structural LaTeX change you cannot otherwise validate) — and prefer the
lightest check possible even then.
